{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795272180,"nonce":"AhSi0YLR4q081vk+STJFVt+8irE=","message":"Where the tok2 gap went. Measured on the x86 NUC.\n\nMethod. perf is blocked on the NUC, so I added a temporary in-process sampler (SIGPROF at 1 kHz, record the interrupted PC, resolve against nm). The same binary ran with the pool and with Immix, three runs each, and I diffed samples per function. The sampler is not committed.\n\nBefore the fix, Immix had about 9% more CPU samples than the pool. The collector and allocator functions explained about a third of that. The rest was spread over compiled Lua code and string functions. Page faults gave the clue: Immix took about 100K faults against 59K for the pool, with system time 0.15 s against 0.10 s.\n\nCause. Immix resized a large block by allocating a new one, copying and freeing the old. The pool calls the system realloc, which on glibc moves a big block with mremap and copies nothing. tok2 grows its big tables by doubling, so Immix touched every new page and copied it again. A Python list of 1.5M items grows the same way.\n\nFix. A block that stays above the medium size is now resized with the system realloc. Faults fell to about 70K and system time to 0.10 s. After the fix the CPU samples are within 1.5% (8042 for Immix, 7930 for the pool over three runs).\n\nWall time in seconds, median of 5 on an idle machine (previous commit, now, pool):\n\n| row | before | now | pool |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| tok2 | 2.21 | 2.14 | 1.99 |\n| w_phase | 1.78 | 1.75 | 1.55 |\n| w_hold | 1.87 | 1.86 | 1.80 |\n| w_grow | 0.342 | 0.335 | 0.343 |\n| binary_trees | 0.180 | 0.174 | 0.153 |\n| tokenize | 0.108 | 0.105 | 0.099 |\n\nThe gain is small, between 1% and 3%.\n\nTried and dropped, no measured effect: keeping the mark stack between collections, and not zeroing collected large blocks.\n\nRemaining difference in the profile, per run: about 50 ms in the allocator and about 35 ms in marking. The allocator entry still saves five registers, because the thread-local access compiles to a call before the linker relaxes it. An inline bump in compiled code removes the call, and it is the larger change.\n\nLanded in 69978bbd. Lua conformance under Immix passes 46 of 46 on the Mac and the NUC. A Python script that grows a 1.5M-item list and a 200K-entry dict prints the same result under both heaps.","files":null}]}